            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Paradise, the original state of perfection, divine sanctuary, and unbroken communion with God, often representing both original creation and eschatological restoration.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 2:8-15\",\"Genesis 3:1-24\",\"Revelation 22:1-5\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A place of agony, intense testing, and willing surrender to God's will (Gethsemane).\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 26:36-46\",\"Mark 14:32-42\",\"Luke 22:39-46\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Intimate love, purity, and the bride or Church (a locked garden).\",\"basis\":[\"Song of Solomon 4:12-16\",\"Song of Solomon 5:1\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The flourishing human soul or the restored people of God, nourished by the Holy Spirit (a watered garden).\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 58:11\",\"Jeremiah 31:12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"The site of burial and subsequent resurrection, bridging death and new life (the garden tomb).\",\"basis\":[\"John 19:41\",\"John 20:15\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The garden motif frames the entire biblical narrative, starting with the Garden of Eden as a protological temple and ending with the garden-city in Revelation. Intermediate gardens like Gethsemane and the garden of the tomb serve as critical transitional sites of Christ's passion and resurrection.\"}",
